  "content": {
    "id": "p39-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "Let $f(x)=ax^2+bx+c$, where $a\\ne0$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p39-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $f\\left(-\\frac{b}{a}\\right)=f(0)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f\\left(-\\frac{b}{a}\\right)=c=f(0)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f\\left(-\\frac{b}{a}\\right)&=a\\left(-\\frac{b}{a}\\right)^2+b\\left(-\\frac{b}{a}\\right)+c\\\\f\\left(-\\frac{b}{a}\\right)&=\\frac{b^2}{a}-\\frac{b^2}{a}+c\\\\f\\left(-\\frac{b}{a}\\right)&=c.\\end{align*}$$\nAlso, $f(0)=c$. Hence $f\\left(-\\frac{b}{a}\\right)=f(0)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p39-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Explain this in terms of the symmetry of the parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The inputs $0$ and $-\\frac{b}{a}$ are symmetric about the axis $x=-\\frac{b}{2a}$, so their function values are equal.",
          "worked": "The axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{b}{2a}$. The midpoint of the two inputs is\n$$\\frac{0+\\left(-\\frac{b}{a}\\right)}{2}=-\\frac{b}{2a}.$$\nThus reflection in this axis takes $(0,f(0))$ to $\\left(-\\frac{b}{a},f\\left(-\\frac{b}{a}\\right)\\right)$. Reflection preserves the $y$-coordinate, so the function values are equal. When $b=0$, the two inputs coincide on the axis.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      }
    ]
  },
